---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/revenue-attribution';
const title = "Shopify email revenue attribution: reconcile credit and lift";
const desc = "Decide whether a flow deserves investment by separating credited orders, realized sales and incremental contribution.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can a UTM match prove incremental revenue?",
    "answer": "No. It links a tracked visit to a reporting rule; a causal claim needs an appropriate comparison."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is a fixed holdout percentage enough?",
    "answer": "No. Sample size depends on baseline outcomes, useful effect size and uncertainty. Plan it before the experiment."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should email and SMS revenue be added?",
    "answer": "Only after order-level reconciliation under an explicit credit policy; overlapping claims can count the same sale twice."
  },
  {
    "question": "What if the test is inconclusive?",
    "answer": "Report the estimate and uncertainty, retain guardrails and decide whether more observations are worth the opportunity cost."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">All operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><article class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Define the question before choosing a window</h2>
    <p>Attribution assigns credit under a rule; it does not establish that a message caused an order. A shorter window changes credit, not the counterfactual. Keep the vendor report with its click/view rules, timezone and settings date. Use it to diagnose message paths; do not add overlapping email and SMS totals into a finance total.</p>
    <h2>Build an order-level reconciliation ledger</h2>
    <p>Export order ID, customer ID where available, currency, order timestamp, credited message and channel, attribution rule, discounts, refunds and order type. Keep one row per order in the sales ledger and a separate touchpoint table. If a message export contains several rows per order, deduplicate before summing sales. Preserve unmatched rows for investigation rather than forcing a match through email address alone.</p>
    <h2>Separate three numbers in the report</h2>
    <p>Show vendor-credited sales under its stated rules; realized net sales under the finance definition; and estimated incremental contribution from a suitable experiment. A UTM match describes a tracked visit, not all sales and not causality. Missing parameters, customer identity changes and different cutoff times can explain disagreement. Record reconciliation differences by cause and leave unresolved value visible.</p>
    <h2>Proposed holdout design</h2>
    <p>Choose one decision, such as whether an additional cart reminder is worthwhile. Randomly assign eligible customers to the existing sequence or the sequence with that reminder. Keep assignment stable at customer level to avoid repeat carts crossing groups. Apply the same purchase observation window to both arms and analyze all assigned eligible customers, including delivery failures. Record other campaigns and SMS exposure; if they differ systematically, the contrast no longer isolates the reminder.</p>
    <h2>Size and interpret the experiment</h2>
    <p>Choose sample size and duration from the baseline outcome, the smallest commercially useful effect and acceptable uncertainty, rather than a universal holdout percentage. Include a complete purchase cycle and allow refunds to mature. Report denominators, estimate and uncertainty; an inconclusive result is not zero lift. Do not stop because one daily result looks favorable. Operational QA can pass before a causal experiment has enough observations.</p>
    <h2>Hypothetical calculation, not a merchant result</h2>
    <p>Suppose 1,000 assigned customers in each arm produce 120 versus 100 purchasers: purchase rates are 12% and 10%, a 2 percentage point difference. That is 20 additional purchasers per 1,000 under this invented example, before uncertainty. It is not evidence that every credited order is incremental. Compare net sales or contribution per assigned customer directly when order values differ.</p>
    <h2>Discounts, refunds and subscription renewals</h2>
    <p>Agree with finance whether the sales field already reflects discounts so they are not subtracted twice. Deduct returns and relevant variable costs consistently; allocate platform costs separately from marginal send cost. Mark automatic renewals separately from discretionary purchases. A renewal after a click is not proof that email created the renewal; test the renewal-related decision separately from a winback offer.</p>
    <h2>A report a finance owner can reproduce</h2>
    <p>Keep the export cutoff, currency policy, refund lag, join rules, duplicate count, unmatched value and methodology version alongside the numbers. Track unsubscribes and complaints as guardrails. When rules change, annotate the series rather than describing the discontinuity as performance growth. Ask Sequenzy or any other finalist to export the order IDs and rule settings needed for this ledger; no reporting capability or result is assumed here.</p>
    <h2>Data evidence and next steps</h2>
    <p>Shopify’s <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Order">Order reference</a> documents purchase and refund data and the default restriction on older order access. The <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Customer">Customer reference</a> describes history and marketing preferences. Checked November 25, 2026; neither establishes what a particular email connector imports. Confirm history coverage before treating missing records as no purchases.</p>
    <p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/guides/revenue-attribution">revenue measurement</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/segmentation">audience rules</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">tool selection</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">cost modeling</a>, or the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a>.</p>
  </article></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
